# G088 — Sort File Records Utility

*v2. See `sort-file-records.md` for prose: file-as-table, sort spec
composes single-axis sorts, stable-sort as contract, missing fields
sort first ascending.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Record Table

[vault-record-table @csv-path
    IN ORDER TO
        present a CSV as a sortable table where clicks on column headers
        compose a multi-key sort spec the user builds up interactively
        and where the underlying sort is stable across every run

    @text  <- [vault[read-string {path: @csv-path}]]
    @table <- [sfr[parse {text: @text, has-header: true}]]
    @spec  <- []

    sync @user-events {
        click @col-name ->
            @idx  <- [sfr[field-index
                            {table: @table, name: @col-name}]]
            @spec <- [{field: @idx, order: "asc", kind: "str"}]
            [sfr[sort-by {table: @table, spec: @spec}]]
            [ui[render-rows {rows: @table:rows}]]

        shift-click @col-name ->
            @idx  <- [sfr[field-index
                            {table: @table, name: @col-name}]]
            @spec <- @spec + {field: @idx, order: "asc", kind: "str"}
            [sfr[sort-by {table: @table, spec: @spec}]]
            [ui[render-rows {rows: @table:rows}]]
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[sfr
    parse-record        {line}
    serialise-record    {record}
    parse               {text, has-header}
    serialise           {table}
    sort-by             {table, spec}                ;; mutates; stable
    field-index         {table, name}                ;; || unknown-field
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: multi-key sort as `where` short-circuit

[sfr[sort-by {table: @t, spec: @spec}]
    IN ORDER TO
        order @t:rows by walking @spec key-by-key
        returning the first non-zero comparison per pair
        and preserving insertion order wherever all keys tie

    for @a, @b in @t:rows
        for @key in @spec
            where compare(@a[@key:field], @b[@key:field],
                          kind: @key:kind, order: @key:order)
                  is non-zero
                -> first such comparison decides
        otherwise
            -> preserve original index order (stable)
]

Stability is not an optimisation — it is the contract that makes
`serialise(sort(parse(text)))` deterministic across runs, which is
what version control, diffs, and audit logs depend on.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: missing fields as `where NULLS FIRST`

[@record:fields at @idx
    where absent
        sorts first in ascending order
        sorts last  in descending order
    otherwise
        compares by declared kind (string or integer)
]

Partial records still belong in the output. Clustering them at the
start (or end) gives the UI a stable place to render "incomplete"
indicators without breaking the total-order invariant.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @sample <- "name,age,score\nAlice,30,85\nBob,25,92\n" +
               "Carol,30,78\nDave,25,88\n"
    @t <- [sfr[parse {text: @sample, has-header: true}]]

    [sfr[sort-by
          {table: @t,
           spec: [{field: 1, order: "asc",  kind: "int"}
                   {field: 2, order: "desc", kind: "int"}]}]]
    -> @t:rows
    ;; -> [Bob,25,92  Dave,25,88  Alice,30,85  Carol,30,78]

    -> [sfr[serialise {table: [sfr[parse {text: @sample,
                                            has-header: true}]]}]]
       is equal to @sample                          ;; -> true (round-trip)
]
